      </section>

      <section class="mt-12">
        <h3 id="harry-potter" class="mb-4 scroll-mt-nav text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 md:text-xl">7. Explore Edinburgh’s Harry Potter Locations</h3>
        <p>If you’re a Harry Potter fan, you’ll find plenty of places around Edinburgh that are connected to J.K. Rowling and the world of Harry Potter. Some are linked to her time writing the books, while others are believed to have inspired names and places from the stories.</p>
        <ul class="mt-8 list-disc space-y-8 pl-5 marker:text-black/40">
          <li class="pl-1">
            <p><strong>Greyfriars Kirkyard:</strong> This atmospheric old cemetery is one of the most interesting stops for Harry Potter fans. As you wander among the gravestones, look out for Thomas Riddell, whose name is often linked to Voldemort, and William McGonagall, which may have inspired Professor McGonagall’s name. These connections are widely believed by fans, although they haven’t been confirmed by Rowling.</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 md:gap-4">
              <ArticleImage
                src={greyfriarsThomasRiddell}
                alt="The Thomas Riddell gravestone at Greyfriars Kirkyard"
                caption="Thomas Riddell’s grave"
                imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover"
              />
              <ArticleImage
                src={greyfriarsMcGonagall}
                alt="The William McGonagall memorial plaque at Greyfriars Kirkyard"
                caption="William McGonagall’s plaque"
                imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover"
              />
            </div>
            <ArticleImage
              src={greyfriarsKirkyard}
              alt="Gravestones at Greyfriars Kirkyard in Edinburgh"
              caption="Greyfriars Kirkyard"
              imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover"
            />
          </li>
          <li class="pl-1">
            <p><strong>The Elephant House:</strong> This famous café on George IV Bridge is often referred to as the birthplace of Harry Potter. J.K. Rowling did spend time writing here in the early days of the series, although she had already started working on the books before visiting. The café reopened in December 2025 after being closed for several years following a fire, so you can once again stop by for a coffee.</p>
            <ArticleImage
              src={elephantHouseInterior}
              alt="Inside The Elephant House café, with wooden tables and windows onto the street"
