              <ArticleImage
                src={elephantHouseDesk}
                alt="A writing desk with books, pumpkins and a guestbook at The Elephant House"
                caption="The writing desk at The Elephant House"
                imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover"
              />
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="pl-1">
            <p><strong>The Balmoral Hotel:</strong> One of the most interesting Harry Potter connections in Edinburgh is found at this luxurious hotel on Princes Street. J.K. Rowling completed the final Harry Potter book, <em>Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows</em>, in Room 552, which has since been renamed the J.K. Rowling Suite. Even if staying there isn’t on your plans, it’s worth spotting the hotel and its famous clock tower as you explore the city.</p>
            <ArticleImage
              src={balmoralHotel}
              alt="The Balmoral Hotel and its clock tower on Princes Street, Edinburgh"
              caption="The Balmoral Hotel on Princes Street"
              imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover"
            />
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-8"><strong>A little extra:</strong> Victoria Street, which we mentioned earlier, is often compared to Diagon Alley because of its colorful curved buildings and magical-looking shopfronts. It’s another lovely stop if you want to add a little more Harry Potter atmosphere to your walk around the Old Town.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12">
        <h3 id="whisky" class="mb-4 scroll-mt-nav text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 md:text-xl">8. Discover Scotland’s Whisky Culture</h3>
        <p>If you’d like to learn more about Scotland’s famous whisky-making tradition, Edinburgh offers a couple of interesting experiences worth checking out. The Scotch Whisky Experience, located just next to Edinburgh Castle, takes you through the whisky-making process and introduces you to different whisky regions across Scotland. Another option is Johnnie Walker Princes Street, where you can explore the world of one of Scotland’s best-known whisky brands through guided tasting experiences and interactive exhibits. Both are great options if you’d like to learn a little more about Scottish whisky during your visit.</p>
