  <section id="faq" class="scroll-mt-nav">
    <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
      <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">FAQ</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="article-prose max-w-3xl text-black/80 [&_p]:mb-5 [&_p:last-child]:mb-0 space-y-6">
      <div id="faq-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">1. How many days do you need in Edinburgh?</h3>
        <p>Two to three days are ideal for exploring Edinburgh’s main attractions, walking through the Old Town and New Town, and enjoying its cafés, shops, and historic streets. If you want to explore at a slower pace or visit places outside the city center, consider staying four days.</p>
      </div>
      <div id="faq-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">2. What is the best time of year to visit Edinburgh?</h3>
        <p>The best time to visit Edinburgh depends on the kind of trip you’re planning. Spring and early fall are great for milder weather and fewer crowds, while summer brings longer days and the famous Edinburgh festivals. Winter is especially charming during the Christmas season, although days are shorter and temperatures are colder.</p>
      </div>
      <div id="faq-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">3. Is Edinburgh an expensive city to visit?</h3>
        <p>Edinburgh can be relatively expensive, especially when it comes to accommodation. Prices tend to increase during August’s festivals and the Christmas and New Year period. However, you can keep your budget under control by booking accommodation in advance, using public transportation, and taking advantage of the city’s many free attractions.</p>
      </div>
      <div id="faq-3" class="scroll-mt-nav">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">4. Is Edinburgh a walkable city?</h3>
        <p>Yes! Edinburgh is a very walkable city, and many of its main attractions are within walking distance of each other. You can easily explore the Old Town, the Royal Mile, Princes Street, and the New Town on foot. Just keep in mind that the city has steep hills, cobblestone streets, and some uneven paths, so comfortable walking shoes are essential.</p>
      </div>
      <div id="faq-4" class="scroll-mt-nav">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">5. Do you need to book Edinburgh Castle tickets in advance?</h3>
        <p>Yes, booking your Edinburgh Castle tickets online in advance is highly recommended, especially during summer and other busy travel periods. Tickets can sell out, and booking ahead helps guarantee your preferred visit. Check the official website for availability and current prices before your trip.</p>
      </div>
      <div id="faq-5" class="scroll-mt-nav">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">6. What are the best free things to do in Edinburgh?</h3>
        <p>Edinburgh has plenty of free attractions to enjoy. You can explore the Royal Mile, wander through Dean Village, admire the views from Calton Hill, or stroll through Princes Street Gardens. The Scottish National Gallery and the National Museum of Scotland are also great options for a free cultural experience. Some special exhibitions or events may require paid tickets.</p>
      </div>
      <div id="faq-6" class="scroll-mt-nav">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">7. What should you pack for a trip to Edinburgh?</h3>
        <p>Edinburgh’s weather can be unpredictable, so it’s best to pack layers that you can easily add or remove throughout the day. Bring a waterproof jacket, comfortable walking shoes, and an umbrella or light raincoat. Even in summer, having an extra layer is a good idea, especially for cooler evenings.</p>
      </div>
      <div id="faq-7" class="scroll-mt-nav">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">8. Where is the best area to stay in Edinburgh?</h3>
        <p>The Old Town and New Town are two of the best areas to stay in Edinburgh, especially if you’re visiting for the first time. The Old Town is ideal for being close to historic landmarks and attractions, while the New Town offers beautiful Georgian architecture, shopping streets, and plenty of restaurants. Both are central and convenient for exploring the city on foot.</p>
      </div>
      <div id="faq-8" class="scroll-mt-nav">
        <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">9. Is Edinburgh worth visiting for a weekend?</h3>
        <p>Absolutely! Edinburgh is a wonderful destination for a weekend city break. With two or three days, you can explore its most famous landmarks, discover charming neighborhoods, visit museums, and enjoy its cafés and restaurants. While you won’t see everything, it’s enough time to experience the city’s highlights and get a feel for its unique atmosphere.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

